# WebSocket (RFC 6455) over std/http: the server's handshake, the
# client's, and the frames after them, written in iyi.
#
# **The server.** Inside `Server.serve`'s block, `WebSocket.upgrade(req)
# { |ws| ... }` answers the request. A request that is a version 13
# handshake - a GET over HTTP/1.1 whose `Upgrade` names `websocket`, whose
# `Connection` holds `upgrade`, and whose `Sec-WebSocket-Key` is 16 bytes
# in base64 - is answered 101 with the key's `Sec-WebSocket-Accept`, and
# the block runs with the connection. Any other request is answered 400
# with why, and a handshake of another version 426 with
# `Sec-WebSocket-Version: 13` (RFC 6455 §4.2.2, §4.4). With `protocols`
# the server names the first of its own list the client offered in
# `Sec-WebSocket-Protocol`, and none when it offered none of them: the
# server's order is the preference, since it is the server that knows
# which it serves best. The connection is closed when the block returns;
# a block that did not close it is closed for, 1000. An `Origin` is not
# checked here: the block reads `req.header("Origin")` before it calls
# `upgrade` when it cares. Closing the listener does not end a connection
# that has been upgraded, and `serve` returns once those have ended, so a
# server that stops closes its WebSockets itself.
#
# **The client.** `WebSocket.connect("ws://host:port/path")` sends the
# handshake through `HTTP.upgrade` with a key of 16 bytes from
# `Random::Secure` and checks what comes back: a 101 whose `Upgrade` and
# `Connection` say websocket, whose `Sec-WebSocket-Accept` is the key's,
# that agrees to no extension, and names no protocol the client did not
# offer. Each of these refused is a `WebSocketError`; a server that does
# not answer 101 is the `HTTPError` `HTTP.upgrade` names it with. `wss://`
# is refused before anything is sent: std/tls speaks `https://`, and a
# connection handed over to another protocol under TLS is not here yet.
#
# **The connection.** `send(text)`, `send_binary(bytes)` and `ping` write
# one frame each: no message is split into fragments on the way out.
# `receive` answers the next whole message, a `Message` whose `data` is
# its bytes, and nil once the close handshake is done. On the way it
# reassembles fragments, with pings and pongs between them; answers a
# ping with its pong; and reads past pongs. `close(code, reason)` sends a
# close and waits up to ten seconds for the peer's. A close the peer
# starts is answered with its own code and ends `receive`; `close_code`
# and `close_reason` say what it carried (1005 when it carried no code,
# 1006 when the connection ended without one).
#
# **What the other end does wrong** fails the connection as §7.1.7 has
# it: a close frame with the code that names the fault is sent, the
# socket is closed, and `receive` answers a `WebSocketError` with that
# code. A frame with a reserved bit set (no extension is ever agreed) or a
# reserved opcode, a client's frame without a mask or a server's with one
# (§5.1), a control frame that is fragmented or longer than 125 bytes
# (§5.5), a continuation with no message to continue or a new message
# before the last one's final frame (§5.4), a 64-bit length with its high
# bit set (§5.2), a close of one byte or with a code no peer may send
# (§7.4) are 1002. Text that is not UTF-8 - checked as the fragments
# arrive, a character split between two of them being whole, and an
# unfinished one at the end not - and a close reason that is not, are
# 1007. A message past `max_message` bytes (16 MiB unless the caller says
# otherwise) is 1009, refused on the length its frame declares, before
# any of it is read or anything is made to hold it: a frame that claims
# 2^62 bytes costs the 14 bytes of its head. A connection that fails
# below the protocol - `SocketError`, `Cancelled`, or the peer gone
# without a close frame (1006) - is closed too, since a frame read part
# way leaves nothing after it that can be trusted to be a frame.
#
# **What a program does wrong** is a panic, before anything is written:
# text that is not UTF-8 handed to `send`, a ping past 125 bytes or a
# close reason past 123, a close code that is not one an endpoint sends
# (1000 to 1003, 1007 to 1014, 3000 to 4999), a caller's header that is
# the handshake's own, a protocol name that is not a token.
#
# Masks come from `Random::Secure`, four fresh bytes a frame (§5.3 asks
# that they not be predictable), and are applied, like the UTF-8 check's
# run over ASCII, eight bytes a time. A message in one frame is read
# straight into the string `receive` answers; fragments into one buffer
# that grows as they come and is let go after the message. Measured here
# (`--release`, one machine, both ends in one process over loopback): 128
# MiB as eight 16 MiB messages from a client to a server, masked and
# unmasked, in 70 ms as binary and 85 ms as text.
#
# Frames are written whole, one at a time per connection: a task that
# sends while another receives (whose pongs and close replies are writes
# too) waits, a millisecond at a time, for the frame being written to
# finish. The runtime refuses a second write to a socket another task's
# write is parked on ("two fibers writing one fd"), and a frame let in
# between would have split the first.
#
# Not here: extensions (permessage-deflate is not offered or accepted),
# TLS, and sending a message in fragments. `bench/std_websocket_exercise.sh`
# runs both halves against each other, against hand-made frames, and
# against Python and Node peers.

module std/websocket

import std/http::{HTTP, Request, Response, Upgraded, HTTPError}
import std/socket::{IyiSocket, SocketError}
import std/digest::{SHA1}
import std/base64::{Base64}
import std/random::{Random}

# What the other end did that RFC 6455 does not allow, or a handshake
# that is not one, as a value: `reason` is the sentence, `code` the close
# code it stands for (1002 a protocol error, 1007 bytes that are not
# UTF-8, 1009 a message too large, 1006 a connection that ended without
# a close). `message` says it is WebSocket's.
pub struct WebSocketError
  getter reason : String
  getter code : Int32

  def initialize(@reason : String, @code : Int32 = 1002)
  end

  def to_s : String
    "WebSocket: " + @reason
  end
end

impl Error for WebSocketError
  def message : String
    "WebSocket: " + @reason
  end
end

# One whole message: its bytes, and whether it came as text (UTF-8,
# checked) or binary.
pub struct Message
  getter data : String

  def initialize(@data : String, @binary : Bool)
  end

  def text? : Bool
    !@binary
  end

  def binary? : Bool
    @binary
  end
end

# One end of a connection whose handshake is done: what `upgrade` hands
# its block and `connect` answers. `WebSocket.new(socket, leftover,
# client)` takes over a socket some other code did the handshake on;
# *leftover* is what arrived after the handshake's head, read first.
pub class WebSocket
  OP_CONTINUATION =  0
  OP_TEXT         =  1
  OP_BINARY       =  2
  OP_CLOSE        =  8
  OP_PING         =  9
  OP_PONG         = 10

  # §1.3's constant, appended to the key before it is hashed.
  GUID = "258EAFA5-E914-47DA-95CA-C5AB0DC85B11"

  # The largest message `receive` takes by default: 16 MiB.
  MAX_MESSAGE = 16777216

  # How long `close` waits for the peer's close, and a client for the
  # server to end the connection after it.
  CLOSE_WAIT_MS = 10000

  # The read buffer's size. A payload past it is read straight into the
  # string it becomes.
  ROOM = 16384

  getter protocol : String?
  @socket : IyiSocket
  @client : Bool
  @max_message : Int32
  @in : Pointer(UInt8)
  @in_room : Int32
  @in_start : Int32
  @in_stop : Int32
  # The fragments of a message so far: its bytes, how many, and its
  # opcode (OP_TEXT or OP_BINARY; 0 between messages).
  @parts : Pointer(UInt8)
  @parts_room : Int32
  @parts_size : Int32
  @parts_op : Int32
  # The UTF-8 check's state between a text message's fragments.
  @utf8 : Int32
  @sent_close : Bool
  @close_code : Int32?
  @close_reason : String
  @writing : Bool

  def initialize(@socket : IyiSocket, leftover : String, @client : Bool, @protocol : String? = nil, @max_message : Int32 = MAX_MESSAGE)
    raise "WebSocket: max_message is a count of bytes, not #{@max_message}" if @max_message < 0
    @in_room = leftover.bytesize > ROOM ? leftover.bytesize : ROOM
    @in = Pointer(UInt8).malloc(@in_room.to_u64)
    @in.copy_from(leftover.to_unsafe, leftover.bytesize)
    @in_start = 0
    @in_stop = leftover.bytesize
    @parts = Pointer(UInt8).malloc(16_u64)
    @parts_room = 0
    @parts_size = 0
    @parts_op = 0
    @utf8 = 0
    @sent_close = false
    @close_code = nil
    @close_reason = ""
    @writing = false
  end

  # The server's half of the handshake, answering *req*: a 101 whose
  # block is handed the connection, or the 400 or 426 that says why the
  # request is not a handshake this server takes. *protocols* are the
  # subprotocols the server speaks, in its order of preference.
  def self.upgrade(req : Request, protocols : Array(String) = [] of String, max_message : Int32 = MAX_MESSAGE, &handler : WebSocket -> Nil) : Response
    protocols.each do |name|
      raise "WebSocket: protocol #{name.inspect} is not a token" unless HTTP.token?(name)
    end
    if refused = WebSocket.refusal(req)
      return refused
    end
    key = HTTP.trim(req.header("Sec-WebSocket-Key") || "")
    chosen = nil.as(String?)
    if offered = req.header("Sec-WebSocket-Protocol")
      names = offered.split(',').map { |part| HTTP.trim(part) }
      protocols.each do |name|
        chosen = name if chosen.nil? && names.includes?(name)
      end
    end
    headers = {"Upgrade" => "websocket", "Connection" => "Upgrade", "Sec-WebSocket-Accept" => WebSocket.accept(key)}
    if name = chosen
      headers["Sec-WebSocket-Protocol"] = name
    end
    Response.upgrade(headers) do |socket, leftover|
      ws = WebSocket.new(socket, leftover, false, chosen, max_message)
      handler.call(ws)
      ws.close unless ws.closed?
      nil
    end
  end

  # Why *req* is not a handshake this server answers 101, as the answer;
  # nil when it is one.
  protected def self.refusal(req : Request) : Response?
    return Response.new(400, "a WebSocket handshake is a GET, not #{req.method}\n") unless req.method == "GET"
    return Response.new(400, "a WebSocket handshake is HTTP/1.1, not #{req.version}\n") unless req.version == "HTTP/1.1"
    upgrade = req.header("Upgrade")
    return Response.new(400, "Upgrade does not name websocket\n") unless upgrade && HTTP.has_token?(upgrade, "websocket")
    connection = req.header("Connection")
    return Response.new(400, "Connection does not hold upgrade\n") unless connection && HTTP.has_token?(connection, "upgrade")
    version = req.header("Sec-WebSocket-Version")
    return Response.new(400, "no Sec-WebSocket-Version\n") unless version
    unless HTTP.trim(version) == "13"
      return Response.new(426, "Sec-WebSocket-Version #{version} is not 13\n", {"Sec-WebSocket-Version" => "13"})
    end
    key = req.header("Sec-WebSocket-Key")
    return Response.new(400, "no Sec-WebSocket-Key\n") unless key
    return Response.new(400, "Sec-WebSocket-Key is not 16 bytes in base64\n") unless WebSocket.key?(HTTP.trim(key))
    nil
  end

  # Whether *key* is what §4.1 has a client send: 16 bytes in base64,
  # which is 22 characters of the alphabet and `==`.
  protected def self.key?(key : String) : Bool
    return false unless key.bytesize == 24
    p = key.to_unsafe
    return false unless p[22] == 61_u8 && p[23] == 61_u8
    i = 0
    while i < 22
      c = p[i]
      letter = (c >= 65_u8 && c <= 90_u8) || (c >= 97_u8 && c <= 122_u8)
      digit = c >= 48_u8 && c <= 57_u8
      return false unless letter || digit || c == 43_u8 || c == 47_u8
      i = i + 1
    end
    true
  end

  # The `Sec-WebSocket-Accept` that answers *key* (§4.2.2): the base64 of
  # the SHA-1 of the key and the GUID.
  def self.accept(key : String) : String
    hash = SHA1.new.update(key).update(GUID).final
    Base64.encode(String.new(20) { |dst| dst.copy_from(hash.to_unsafe, 20) })
  end

  # The client's half: the handshake to a `ws://` URL, and the connection
  # it opens. *headers* go with the request (an `Origin`, a cookie); the
  # handshake's own are refused. *protocols* are offered in their order.
  def self.connect(url : String, headers : Hash(String, String) = {} of String => String, protocols : Array(String) = [] of String, max_message : Int32 = MAX_MESSAGE) : WebSocket | Cancelled | SocketError | HTTPError | WebSocketError
    headers.each do |name, _|
      ["Upgrade", "Connection", "Sec-WebSocket-Key", "Sec-WebSocket-Version", "Sec-WebSocket-Protocol", "Sec-WebSocket-Extensions"].each do |own|
        raise "WebSocket: #{name} is the handshake's own header" if HTTP.same_name?(name, own)
      end
    end
    protocols.each do |name|
      raise "WebSocket: protocol #{name.inspect} is not a token" unless HTTP.token?(name)
    end
    colon = url.index(':')
    scheme = colon ? url[0, colon].downcase : ""
    return WebSocketError.new("wss:// is not here yet: a connection handed over under TLS; use ws://") if scheme == "wss"
    return WebSocketError.new("not a ws:// URL: #{url.inspect}") unless scheme == "ws" && colon
    target = "http" + url[colon, url.bytesize - colon]
    nonce = Pointer(UInt8).malloc(16_u64)
    Random::Secure.fill(nonce, 16)
    key = Base64.encode(String.new(16) { |dst| dst.copy_from(nonce, 16) })
    sent = headers.dup
    sent["Sec-WebSocket-Key"] = key
    sent["Sec-WebSocket-Version"] = "13"
    sent["Sec-WebSocket-Protocol"] = protocols.join(", ") unless protocols.empty?
    up = HTTP.upgrade(target, "websocket", sent)!
    agreed = WebSocket.agreed(up, key, protocols)
    if agreed.is_a?(WebSocketError)
      up.socket.close
      return agreed
    end
    WebSocket.new(up.socket, up.leftover, true, agreed, max_message)
  end

  # The protocol a server's 101 agreed to (nil for none), or why the
  # answer is not one to the handshake *key* offering *protocols* (§4.1).
  protected def self.agreed(up : Upgraded, key : String, protocols : Array(String)) : String | Nil | WebSocketError
    answer = up.response
    upgrade = answer.header("Upgrade")
    return WebSocketError.new("the server's 101 does not upgrade to websocket") unless upgrade && HTTP.has_token?(upgrade, "websocket")
    connection = answer.header("Connection")
    return WebSocketError.new("the server's 101 has no Connection: Upgrade") unless connection && HTTP.has_token?(connection, "upgrade")
    accepted = answer.header("Sec-WebSocket-Accept")
    return WebSocketError.new("the server's 101 has no Sec-WebSocket-Accept") unless accepted
    return WebSocketError.new("the server's Sec-WebSocket-Accept #{accepted.inspect} does not answer the key sent") unless HTTP.trim(accepted) == accept(key)
    if extensions = answer.header("Sec-WebSocket-Extensions")
      return WebSocketError.new("the server agreed to extensions #{extensions.inspect}, and none were offered")
    end
    if named = answer.header("Sec-WebSocket-Protocol")
      name = HTTP.trim(named)
      return WebSocketError.new("the server chose protocol #{name.inspect}, which was not offered") unless protocols.includes?(name)
      return name
    end
    nil
  end

  # Whether the connection is closed: after the close handshake, after a
  # failure, and after `close`.
  def closed? : Bool
    @socket.closed?
  end

  # The code the connection closed with: the peer's close's (1005 when it
  # had none), the one this end failed it with, or 1006 when it ended
  # without a close. Nil while it is open.
  def close_code : Int32?
    @close_code
  end

  # The reason the peer's close carried; "" for none.
  def close_reason : String
    @close_reason
  end

  # *text* as one text message. It must be UTF-8.
  def send(text : String) : Nil | Cancelled | SocketError | WebSocketError
    raise "WebSocket: send's text is not UTF-8; send_binary sends bytes" unless WebSocket.utf8_scan(text.to_unsafe, text.bytesize, 0) == 0
    write_frame(OP_TEXT, text)
  end

  # *data* as one binary message.
  def send_binary(data : String) : Nil | Cancelled | SocketError | WebSocketError
    write_frame(OP_BINARY, data)
  end

  # A ping, which the peer answers with a pong `receive` reads past.
  def ping(data : String = "") : Nil | Cancelled | SocketError | WebSocketError
    raise "WebSocket: a ping carries at most 125 bytes, not #{data.bytesize}" if data.bytesize > 125
    write_frame(OP_PING, data)
  end

  # The next whole message, or nil when the connection is closed: the
  # close handshake is done, or the connection was already closed.
  def receive : Message | Nil | Cancelled | SocketError | WebSocketError
    return nil if @socket.closed?
    while true
      got = read_frame
      case got
      in Message
        return got
      in Bool
        return nil unless got
      in Cancelled
        abandon
        return got
      in SocketError
        abandon
        return got
      in WebSocketError
        return got
      end
    end
    nil
  end

  # The close handshake from this end: a close with *code* and *reason*,
  # then the peer's, read past whatever messages it sent first, for up
  # to ten seconds. Nil once it is done, or when the connection was
  # already closed.
  def close(code : Int32 = 1000, reason : String = "") : Nil | Cancelled | SocketError | WebSocketError
    raise "WebSocket: close code #{code} is not one an endpoint sends: 1000-1003, 1007-1014 or 3000-4999" unless WebSocket.close_code?(code)
    raise "WebSocket: a close reason is at most 123 bytes, not #{reason.bytesize}" if reason.bytesize > 123
    raise "WebSocket: the close reason is not UTF-8" unless WebSocket.utf8_scan(reason.to_unsafe, reason.bytesize, 0) == 0
    return nil if @socket.closed?
    unless @sent_close
      @sent_close = true
      wrote = write_frame(OP_CLOSE, WebSocket.close_payload(code, reason))
      unless wrote.nil?
        abandon
        return wrote
      end
    end
    @socket.read_timeout_ms = CLOSE_WAIT_MS
    while true
      got = read_frame
      case got
      in Message
        next
      in Bool
        return nil unless got
      in Cancelled
        abandon
        return got
      in SocketError
        abandon
        return got
      in WebSocketError
        return got
      end
    end
    nil
  end

  # Whether *code* is one a close frame may carry (§7.4): 1000 to 1003,
  # 1007 to 1014 (1012 to 1014 as IANA's registry added them), and the
  # ranges for libraries and applications. 1004 is reserved, and 1005,
  # 1006 and 1015 are the codes an endpoint reports, never sends.
  def self.close_code?(code : Int32) : Bool
    (code >= 1000 && code <= 1003) || (code >= 1007 && code <= 1014) || (code >= 3000 && code <= 4999)
  end

  protected def self.close_payload(code : Int32, reason : String) : String
    String.new(2 + reason.bytesize) do |dst|
      dst[0] = (code >> 8).to_u8
      dst[1] = (code & 255).to_u8
      (dst + 2).copy_from(reason.to_unsafe, reason.bytesize)
    end
  end

  # One frame of *data*, written whole: masked from a client, bare from a
  # server (§5.1, §5.3). Another task's frame being written is waited
  # for.
  private def write_frame(opcode : Int32, data : String) : Nil | Cancelled | SocketError | WebSocketError
    return WebSocketError.new("the connection is closed", @close_code || 1006) if @socket.closed?
    return WebSocketError.new("the connection is closing; a close has been sent", 1000) if @sent_close && opcode != OP_CLOSE
    n = data.bytesize
    extra = n < 126 ? 0 : (n < 65536 ? 2 : 8)
    head = 2 + extra + (@client ? 4 : 0)
    raise "WebSocket: a message of #{n} bytes and its frame's head are past the 2147483647 bytes a string holds" if n > 2147483647 - head
    masked = @client ? 128 : 0
    frame = String.new(head + n) do |dst|
      dst[0] = (128 | opcode).to_u8
      if extra == 0
        dst[1] = (masked | n).to_u8
      elsif extra == 2
        dst[1] = (masked | 126).to_u8
        dst[2] = (n >> 8).to_u8
        dst[3] = (n & 255).to_u8
      else
        dst[1] = (masked | 127).to_u8
        dst[2] = 0_u8
        dst[3] = 0_u8
        dst[4] = 0_u8
        dst[5] = 0_u8
        dst[6] = (n >> 24).to_u8
        dst[7] = ((n >> 16) & 255).to_u8
        dst[8] = ((n >> 8) & 255).to_u8
        dst[9] = (n & 255).to_u8
      end
      (dst + head).copy_from(data.to_unsafe, n)
      if @client
        key = 0_u32
        Random::Secure.fill(pointerof(key).as(UInt8*), 4)
        (dst + (head - 4)).as(UInt32*).value = key
        WebSocket.mask(dst + head, n, key)
      end
    end
    while @writing
      sleep(1)!
    end
    @writing = true
    wrote = @socket.write(frame)
    @writing = false
    return wrote unless wrote.is_a?(Int32)
    nil
  end

  # *count* bytes at *p* XORed with *key*'s four bytes in turn, from the
  # first (§5.3): eight bytes a time, then one.
  protected def self.mask(p : Pointer(UInt8), count : Int32, key : UInt32) : Nil
    return if key == 0_u32
    # The key's bytes twice over, in memory order either way round.
    word = key.to_u64 | key.to_u64.unsafe_shl(32)
    i = 0
    while i + 8 <= count
      (p + i).as(UInt64*).value = (p + i).as(UInt64*).value ^ word
      i = i + 8
    end
    k32 = key
    k = pointerof(k32).as(UInt8*)
    while i < count
      p[i] = p[i] ^ k[i & 3]
      i = i + 1
    end
  end

  # The UTF-8 check's state after the *count* bytes at *p*, from *state*:
  # 0 between characters; the continuation bytes still owed and the range
  # the next must fall in (§3 of RFC 3629: no overlong forms, no
  # surrogates, nothing past U+10FFFF) packed into one number otherwise;
  # -1 once a byte is wrong. A text message is whole when it ends at 0.
  protected def self.utf8_scan(p : Pointer(UInt8), count : Int32, state : Int32) : Int32
    return -1 if state < 0
    need = state & 255
    low = (state >> 8) & 255
    high = state >> 16
    i = 0
    while i < count
      if need == 0
        # Eight ASCII bytes a time between characters.
        while i + 8 <= count && ((p + i).as(UInt64*).value & 0x8080808080808080_u64) == 0_u64
          i = i + 8
        end
        break if i >= count
        b = p[i].to_i32
        i = i + 1
        if b < 0x80
          next
        elsif b >= 0xC2 && b <= 0xDF
          need = 1
          low = 0x80
          high = 0xBF
        elsif b == 0xE0
          need = 2
          low = 0xA0
          high = 0xBF
        elsif b == 0xED
          need = 2
          low = 0x80
          high = 0x9F
        elsif b >= 0xE1 && b <= 0xEF
          need = 2
          low = 0x80
          high = 0xBF
        elsif b == 0xF0
          need = 3
          low = 0x90
          high = 0xBF
        elsif b >= 0xF1 && b <= 0xF3
          need = 3
          low = 0x80
          high = 0xBF
        elsif b == 0xF4
          need = 3
          low = 0x80
          high = 0x8F
        else
          return -1
        end
      else
        b = p[i].to_i32
        i = i + 1
        return -1 if b < low || b > high
        need = need - 1
        low = 0x80
        high = 0xBF
      end
    end
    need == 0 ? 0 : need | (low << 8) | (high << 16)
  end

  # The next frame, handled: a whole message; true for a frame that is
  # part of one, a ping answered or a pong; false for the close that ends
  # the connection. A fault fails the connection and is answered.
  private def read_frame : Message | Bool | Cancelled | SocketError | WebSocketError
    want(2)!
    b0 = @in[@in_start].to_i32
    b1 = @in[@in_start + 1].to_i32
    fin = (b0 & 128) != 0
    opcode = b0 & 15
    masked = (b1 & 128) != 0
    short = b1 & 127
    return fail(1002, "reserved bits set in a frame, and no extension was agreed that uses them") if (b0 & 112) != 0
    unless opcode <= OP_BINARY || (opcode >= OP_CLOSE && opcode <= OP_PONG)
      return fail(1002, "opcode #{opcode} is reserved")
    end
    if @client
      return fail(1002, "a server's frame is masked") if masked
    else
      return fail(1002, "a client's frame is not masked") unless masked
    end
    control = opcode >= OP_CLOSE
    if control
      return fail(1002, "a control frame is fragmented") unless fin
      return fail(1002, "a control frame claims more than 125 bytes") if short > 125
    end
    extra = short == 126 ? 2 : (short == 127 ? 8 : 0)
    head = 2 + extra + (masked ? 4 : 0)
    want(head)!
    p = @in + @in_start
    length = short.to_u64
    if short == 126
      length = (p[2].to_u64 << 8) | p[3].to_u64
    elsif short == 127
      return fail(1002, "a 64-bit length with its high bit set") if (p[2] & 128_u8) != 0_u8
      length = 0_u64
      j = 2
      while j < 10
        length = length.unsafe_shl(8) | p[j].to_u64
        j = j + 1
      end
    end
    key = masked ? (p + (head - 4)).as(UInt32*).value : 0_u32
    @in_start = @in_start + head
    return control_frame(opcode, length.to_i32, key) if control
    if opcode == OP_CONTINUATION
      return fail(1002, "a continuation frame with no message to continue") if @parts_op == 0
    else
      return fail(1002, "a new message before the last one's final frame") if @parts_op != 0
    end
    kind = opcode == OP_CONTINUATION ? @parts_op : opcode
    total = @parts_size.to_u64 + length
    # Refused on the length the frame declares, before a byte of it is
    # read or anything is made to hold it.
    return fail(1009, "a message past the #{@max_message} bytes this end takes") if total > @max_message.to_u64
    n = length.to_i32
    if opcode != OP_CONTINUATION && fin
      # A message in one frame, read straight into the string it is.
      failed = nil.as(Cancelled | SocketError | WebSocketError | Nil)
      data = String.new(n) { |dst| failed = take_into(dst, n, key) }
      if fault = failed
        return fault
      end
      if kind == OP_TEXT && WebSocket.utf8_scan(data.to_unsafe, n, 0) != 0
        return fail(1007, "a text message that is not UTF-8")
      end
      return Message.new(data, kind == OP_BINARY)
    end
    make_room(total.to_i32)
    take_into(@parts + @parts_size, n, key)!
    if kind == OP_TEXT
      @utf8 = WebSocket.utf8_scan(@parts + @parts_size, n, @utf8)
      return fail(1007, "a text message that is not UTF-8") if @utf8 < 0
    end
    @parts_size = total.to_i32
    @parts_op = kind
    return true unless fin
    return fail(1007, "a text message that ends inside a character") if kind == OP_TEXT && @utf8 != 0
    size = @parts_size
    parts = @parts
    data = String.new(size) { |dst| dst.copy_from(parts, size) }
    # The room a large message took is not kept past it.
    @parts = Pointer(UInt8).malloc(16_u64)
    @parts_room = 0
    @parts_size = 0
    @parts_op = 0
    @utf8 = 0
    Message.new(data, kind == OP_BINARY)
  end

  # Room in the fragments for *total* bytes, which is within the limit.
  private def make_room(total : Int32) : Nil
    return if total <= @parts_room
    room = @parts_room.to_i64 * 2
    room = total.to_i64 if room < total.to_i64
    room = @max_message.to_i64 if room > @max_message.to_i64
    if @parts_room == 0
      @parts = Pointer(UInt8).malloc(room.to_u64)
    else
      @parts = @parts.realloc(room.to_u64)
    end
    @parts_room = room.to_i32
  end

  # A ping, pong or close of *count* bytes, which are 125 at most.
  private def control_frame(opcode : Int32, count : Int32, key : UInt32) : Bool | Cancelled | SocketError | WebSocketError
    want(count)!
    start = @in + @in_start
    payload = String.new(count) { |dst| dst.copy_from(start, count) }
    @in_start = @in_start + count
    WebSocket.mask(payload.to_unsafe, count, key)
    if opcode == OP_PING
      write_frame(OP_PONG, payload)! unless @sent_close
      return true
    end
    return true if opcode == OP_PONG
    code = 1005
    reason = ""
    return fail(1002, "a close frame of one byte; a code is two") if count == 1
    if count >= 2
      code = (payload.to_unsafe[0].to_i32 << 8) | payload.to_unsafe[1].to_i32
      return fail(1002, "close code #{code} is not one a peer sends") unless WebSocket.close_code?(code)
      reason = payload[2, count - 2]
      return fail(1007, "a close reason that is not UTF-8") unless WebSocket.utf8_scan(reason.to_unsafe, reason.bytesize, 0) == 0
    end
    @close_code = code
    @close_reason = reason
    unless @sent_close
      # Its code echoed (§5.5.1), and nothing more.
      @sent_close = true
      write_frame(OP_CLOSE, count >= 2 ? payload[0, 2] : "")
    end
    finish
    false
  end

  # The end of the connection after the close handshake: a server closes
  # it at once (§7.1.1); a client waits for the server to, so that the
  # wait in TIME_WAIT is the server's, and closes it itself after
  # CLOSE_WAIT_MS.
  private def finish : Nil
    linger(@client ? CLOSE_WAIT_MS : 0)
    @socket.close
  end

  # Reads, and drops, until the peer closes, a read fails, or nothing
  # comes for *ms*: a socket closed with unread bytes in it is reset
  # rather than ended, and the reset can overtake the close frame written
  # just before it, which the peer then never reads. With 0 only what has
  # already arrived is read. A megabyte at most.
  private def linger(ms : Int32) : Nil
    return if @socket.closed?
    @socket.read_timeout_ms = ms
    drained = 0
    while drained < 1048576
      got = @socket.read(@in, @in_room)
      return unless got.is_a?(Int32) && got > 0
      drained = drained + got
    end
  end

  # Fails the connection (§7.1.7): a close with *code* if none has been
  # sent, and the socket closed. A client waits for the server to end the
  # connection first, as a server answers a close by doing; a server
  # reads past what has arrived and closes at once, since a client waits
  # for the server to close and both would wait out CLOSE_WAIT_MS. The
  # error says why.
  private def fail(code : Int32, reason : String) : WebSocketError
    unless @sent_close || @socket.closed?
      @sent_close = true
      wrote = write_frame(OP_CLOSE, WebSocket.close_payload(code, reason.bytesize <= 123 ? reason : ""))
      linger(@client ? CLOSE_WAIT_MS : 0) if wrote.nil?
    end
    @close_code = code if @close_code.nil?
    @socket.close
    WebSocketError.new(reason, code)
  end

  # The connection given up on below the protocol: closed, 1006.
  private def abandon : Nil
    @close_code = 1006 if @close_code.nil?
    @socket.close
  end

  # At least *count* bytes in the read buffer, which holds them.
  private def want(count : Int32) : Nil | Cancelled | SocketError | WebSocketError
    while @in_stop - @in_start < count
      if @in_start > 0
        # Down, front to back: no byte is written before it is read.
        held = @in_stop - @in_start
        @in.copy_from(@in + @in_start, held)
        @in_start = 0
        @in_stop = held
      end
      got = @socket.read(@in + @in_stop, @in_room - @in_stop)!
      return ended if got == 0
      @in_stop = @in_stop + got
    end
    nil
  end

  # *count* bytes of payload into *dst*, unmasked with *key*: what the
  # buffer holds, then straight from the socket, a buffer at a time while
  # what is left is smaller than one.
  private def take_into(dst : Pointer(UInt8), count : Int32, key : UInt32) : Nil | Cancelled | SocketError | WebSocketError
    have = @in_stop - @in_start
    have = count if have > count
    dst.copy_from(@in + @in_start, have)
    @in_start = @in_start + have
    done = have
    while done < count
      left = count - done
      if left >= @in_room
        got = @socket.read(dst + done, left)!
        return ended if got == 0
        done = done + got
      else
        @in_start = 0
        @in_stop = 0
        got = @socket.read(@in, @in_room)!
        return ended if got == 0
        @in_stop = got
        piece = got < left ? got : left
        (dst + done).copy_from(@in, piece)
        @in_start = piece
        done = done + piece
      end
    end
    WebSocket.mask(dst, count, key)
    nil
  end

  # The peer gone without a close frame (§7.1.5): 1006.
  private def ended : WebSocketError
    abandon
    WebSocketError.new("the connection ended without a close frame", 1006)
  end
end
